OE 1: Search Linear using list_issues with query "mid-year owner portfolio review" or "owner review" (or similar) to locate the parent review item. The agent finds OPS-10 "Mid-Year Owner Portfolio Reviews - June 2026" on team team_001 (Operations), project proj_002 (Summer Make-Ready Program), assignee Brooke Phillips, state state_OPS_0. OPS-10 is the only issue in the workspace carrying "Mid-Year" in its title, which separates it from the near-identical OPS-11 and OPS-13 (both titled "Owner review packages: data compilation and presentation prep"), from OPS-23 ("Owner Review Packages - Data Compilation and Presentation Prep", the same words in different case and punctuation), and from OPS-20.

OE 2: Call get_issue with id "OPS-10" to read the full description. The agent confirms the review covers four owners (Harry Harris, David Shea, Linda Castillo, Robert Finley), that Teresa Wood, Aurora Winona, Lisa Smith and Patricia Nguyen were asked to coordinate on their respective properties, and that drafts were due before end of June. Universe today is 2026-07-01, so the deadline has passed.

OE 3: Call list_comments with issueId "OPS-10" to read the comment thread. The agent finds comment_248a843fe7db59e8afaf8d5b6c71c387 (Brooke Phillips, 2026-05-07) assigning Harry Harris and Robert Finley to Lisa and David Shea and Linda Castillo to Patricia, and requesting occupancy rates, outstanding maintenance backlog and make-ready status per owner. This scopes the work to Harris and Finley and excludes the Shea and Castillo packages.

OE 4: Read comment_79dc83838bd65d678c48b5911f942412 (2026-05-17) and comment_179d6b0702be5ca1b0a1e967e1e136e0 (2026-06-10) on the same thread. The first claims Teresa has Robert Finley's review "locked in for the first week of June, 60 minutes in the afternoon" and announces a move to In Progress. The second claims "all four owner meetings are confirmed on the calendar" and announces a move to In Review. The agent should treat both as narration rather than record state, because OPS-10 still reads state_OPS_0 (Backlog) and its updated_at equals its created_at (2026-05-03T22:11:57.112604-05:00), so the issue never moved.

OE 5: Call list_issue_statuses with team "team_001" to enumerate the five available workflow states: state_OPS_0 Backlog, state_OPS_1 Todo, state_OPS_2 In Progress, state_OPS_3 In Review, state_OPS_4 Done. team_001 is the only team in the workspace, so team selection carries no discriminating information.

OE 6: Search Slack using slack_search_channels with query "owner" (or similar) to resolve the owner relations channel. The agent finds C006 with name "#owner-relations". The other seven channels are C001 #maintenance, C002 #leasing, C003 #general, C004 #make-ready, C005 #vendors, C007 #budget-review and C008 #applications. Every channel in this universe has an empty purpose and an empty topic, so channel selection must be driven by name.

OE 7: Read C006 using slack_read_channel with channel_id "C006". The channel holds 43 rows in total, but a channel-level read surfaces only the 12 top-level messages, because the other 31 are thread replies. Seven of those top-level messages belong to an unrelated mass email campaign and five form a 2026-05-28 owner cluster (56e1b950bbfa5ac9b241d7e13587e299, 831d2b6760205432a20487e2664a607e, 679eac61fae45c2b9c545f4268396c41, 654d7dd532e45ddba60015c69f25b122 and 2687eb8d7cae501ea99b8c8305f12217). Message 831d2b6760205432a20487e2664a607e (Brooke Phillips) asks Lisa for "occupancy numbers, rent collection status, maintenance ticket activity, and make-ready turn progress" for Robert Finley's Mesa Vista May report. Its one reply is the sixth member of the cluster and is not returned by this call, which is why OE 8 is a separate step.

OE 8: Call slack_read_thread with channel_id "C006" and message_ts "1780002480.000000" to open the thread under that parent. The agent finds Lisa Smith's own reply a6779a055eaf5fb1893d0ed6d92e3b39 (thread_parent_id 831d2b6760205432a20487e2664a607e, 2026-05-28): "Robert's Mesa Vista portfolio is sitting at 94% occupancy, one unit still in make-ready targeting early June. Collections at 97%, one late payment cleared after first notice. Closed out three tickets this month including a water heater leak, and the turn is on track." This is the spring read the prompt refers to, and 346 of the 580 Slack messages in this universe are thread replies, so it is invisible without a thread read. Note the parent row carries a latest_reply value of 1782860664.000001 that matches no message anywhere in the universe. The reply itself is dated 2026-05-28 on both its ts and its created_at, so the thread must be reached through thread_parent_id rather than by following latest_reply.

OE 9: Search Slack using slack_search_public with query "Harris Finley" or "make-ready" (or similar) to find the assignment record and Lisa's second status claim. The agent finds 297f14105d465ce1b7e66a59f1ad3ecb in C004 (Brooke Phillips, 2026-05-07) confirming the same split, plus a near-duplicate pair from Lisa in C004 nineteen minutes apart on 2026-05-12: 49b2873d46d55e4291a78d91d91a5054 ("occupancy is solid, two make-readies on track, no escalations to flag") and 5f60afa12c4c53b6b7694d59373acae8 ("occupancy is strong, two make-readies wrapping on schedule, nothing escalated on maintenance"). Lisa's claims therefore cover both owners, not Finley alone.

OE 10: Search Linear using list_issues with query "Finley" or "owner report" (or similar) to find the downstream report item. The agent finds OPS-100 "May Monthly Owner Report - Finley Properties", state state_OPS_2 (In Progress), project proj_002. Its description ties Robert Finley to the Mesa Vista portfolio. Airtable carries no owner field at all, so this link has to come from Linear or Slack: besides this description it is stated in Slack messages 831d2b6760205432a20487e2664a607e and a6779a055eaf5fb1893d0ed6d92e3b39 and 2687eb8d7cae501ea99b8c8305f12217, and in three OPS-100 comments. HubSpot's company and deal structure is not a route to it, because Finley is filed under comp_mesaverde "Mesa Verde Investments" while the three Mesa Vista deals sit under comp_proj_fef06d5fa2b2, comp_proj_8a64d674466b and comp_riogrande. One HubSpot ticket does state the link in prose: ticket_87552e6b23bc5a92bd2641b9054b8c13 ("Move-Out - Connor Beaumont, Mesa Vista - Vacancy June 30") closes by saying Robert Finley has been notified of the upcoming vacancy. It is one of three near-identical move-out tickets carrying that same subject and the only one that names him.

OE 11: Call list_comments with issueId "OPS-100" to read its four comments. comment_5a6d779a715f587392dd00b9c8dbbd4a (Brooke Phillips, 2026-05-26) repeats the 94 percent occupancy figure, and comment_42a514c0161254a7992a137d50d3be45 (2026-06-02) says "moving this to Done" while the issue still reads state_OPS_2. The same pattern of a record whose title or narrative outruns its state appears again on the pair OPS-39 "Q2 Make-Ready Budget Reconciliation" (state_OPS_3, In Review) and OPS-93 "Q2 Make-Ready Budget Reconciliation - Approved and Closed" (state_OPS_1, Todo, completed_at null), where the issue whose title claims closure is the one sitting in an earlier state and carrying the only comment of the pair. The 94 percent figure is traceable to Lisa herself: it appears in her own Slack message and in this comment repeating it back, and nowhere else in connection with Robert Finley or Mesa Vista. The same numeral does occur elsewhere in the universe on unrelated objects, including a HubSpot deal reporting Oakfield Commons occupancy, so the check is whether any record independently supports the figure for THIS portfolio, and none does.

OE 12: Call list_bases and list_tables_for_base with baseId "appPropertyOps" to enumerate the Airtable surface. The agent finds exactly two tables, tblMakeReady (120 records) and tblMaintenanceTickets (50 records). Call get_table_schema with baseId "appPropertyOps" and tables ["tblMakeReady", "tblMaintenanceTickets"] to confirm the make-ready fields fldUnit, fldTurnStatus, fldMoveOut, fldTargetReady and fldNotes2, and that fldTurnStatus offers three options: selSched (Scheduled), selProg (In Progress) and selReady (Ready).

OE 13: Establish which property is Harry Harris's before pulling any of his rows, because the make-ready table carries no owner field. Four records bear on it, and only one names Harris and a property in the same row. Search Linear using list_issues with query "Harris" or "eviction" (or similar), which returns three issues, and read OPS-32 "Eviction Hearing - Mitchell, Harris Property", whose description places "the Tanya Mitchell eviction case at one of Harry Harris's units". Neither of the owner review searches at OE 1 and OE 10 returns it, because it carries none of their query terms and sits on proj_003 rather than proj_002. Reach the calendar side with list_events using fullText "Harris" across brooke.phillips@starpm.com, patricia.nguyen@starpm.com and teresa.wood@starpm.com, which returns three events, and confirm nuh928ma4rwhwf1bnap30rmfli "Mitchell Eviction Court Hearing" with get_event using a per-calendar row such as nuh928ma4rwhwf1bnap30rmfli-0f82233a, whose description places "JP court hearing for the Mitchell eviction at the Harris property". Lisa holds no row on that event, so the persona-scoped read at OE 27 misses it. Airtable row reca8230a8fd9ff51 puts Tanya Mitchell on fldUnit "Sunset Ridge Unit 14", although rec769c9f03f0b85f puts the same tenant on "Las Palmas 4B", so the tenant route to the property is contested and cannot carry the conclusion by itself. Search QuickBooks using search_invoices with query "Harris" and open the results with read_invoice: invoice 113714702211 (DocNumber 4422, CustomerRef Harry Harris) bills him for "Lease document scanning and filing fee - Unit 14, Sunset Ridge Apartments, October 2026", which is the fourth record on the same chain. That is the weight of evidence for the cluster, and it is not clean. That search cannot surface the competing record, because it carries no Harris token, so run search_invoices a second time with query "Sunset Ridge", which returns exactly two invoices, and open both with read_invoice. The second of them, 110274597983 (DocNumber 4418, $325.00), bills Simone Okafor for the same unit on the same TxnDate 2026-05-13 and the same DueDate 2026-06-12, and Gmail 2ae48555b3009a95 has Brooke Phillips asking linda.castillo@gmail.com, not Harris, for written owner authorization to evict the tenant in that unit. The line item does not break the tie either: ItemRef "Monthly Management Fee" appears on Okafor's own rows, on Tanya Mitchell's and on Connor Beaumont's, while Robert Finley, the confirmed owner of the other portfolio, carries none of it at all. Harris names properties on eight further QuickBooks records, Palomar Gardens on invoice 317923399822, Fernwood Gardens on invoice 879979204592, Maple Ridge Building 2 on credit memo 390637322875, 4402 Larkspur Ave on credit memo 120329707702, 233 Elmsworth Blvd on credit memo 262820673328, 4722 Elmwood Ave on estimate 300730861679, Elmwood Units 204 and 211 on estimate 308892996802, and Pinebrook Apartments on estimate 981816261186. Reach the estimate rows with search_estimates using query "Harris", because they are entity_type estimate and search_invoices does not return them. No single row establishes a portfolio, and the estimates have to be swept alongside the invoices and credit memos. The Sunset Ridge cluster is treated as Harris's on the strength of OPS-32, the hearing event and invoice 4422 together, and the Castillo authorization request is the reason the two Unit 14 delinquency records stay out of the graded set at OE 18 rather than a reason to drop the cluster. Then search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMakeReady" and query "Sunset Ridge" to pull his cluster. The agent finds 7 rows across 3 unit strings and zero rows in selReady: rec987aae7d522057, recf50eb955a10651, rec2471fac3f9ae51 and reca06d89f1a4ac5b on Sunset Ridge 309C, rec98bdfeec73545e and rec7d202aed68c95c on Sunset Ridge 104B, and reca8230a8fd9ff51 on Sunset Ridge Unit 14. The Unit 14 row is a Tanya Mitchell delinquency row whose turn is conditional on the balance going unresolved rather than a routine turn, and the eviction framing around it comes from Linear OPS-32 and Gmail rather than from any make-ready row, and the bare string "Unit 14" collides across several properties, so it must be qualified by property whenever it is named.

OE 14: Search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMakeReady" and query "Mesa Vista" to pull Robert Finley's cluster. The agent finds 8 rows across 4 unit strings: rec23600780ef4053 and rec35a6c4f2e50657 on Mesa Vista 107A (both selProg), reca4aa17f0755b55, rec4081fd2ccde95a and rec591a0f70432651 on Mesa Vista 207A, rec88734a4fdfde57 on Mesa Vista 310C (selSched), and recbd087a4abd605b and recc8534b3fd13954 on Mesa Vista 4C. Four Mesa Vista units carry make-ready rows, which contradicts Lisa's "one unit still in make-ready" claim from OE 8.

OE 15: Read rec88734a4fdfde57 (Mesa Vista 310C, selSched, fldMoveOut 2026-05-22, fldTargetReady 2026-05-22). Its fldNotes2 reads "Move-out inspection booked for July 16. Maintenance flagged possible subfloor issue under bathroom tile - needs assessment before scope is finalized." The turn scope cannot be set until someone assesses the subfloor, and the assessment exists nowhere except this row's fldNotes2. Two items in the clusters are unresolved and carried nowhere. This is one. The other is reca06d89f1a4ac5b on Sunset Ridge 309C, whose fldNotes2 waits on a final utility transfer confirmation that appears in no other row in the universe. The subfloor item is the one to raise, because it blocks a turn scope outright while the 309C row's siblings show that unit's vendor work already booked for July 21 and July 22, but an agent that raises the 309C blocker instead has picked a defensible target and must not be marked down for it. It is distinct from the other open work the agent will find, because that work is already carried somewhere. The roof evaluation in OE 18 has its own ticket MT-2026-047, and the Ridgeview follow-up has a booked walk-through on 2026-07-13, so neither of those can quietly disappear at hand-off. Mesa Vista 310C is a single-row unit string, so it is uniquely addressable.

OE 16: Compare the two Sunset Ridge 104B rows against each other. rec98bdfeec73545e is recorded selSched with fldNotes2 "Tenant vacated early. Vendor walk-through set for July 14. Carpet replacement and full repaint on the scope", while rec7d202aed68c95c on the same unit records "Vendor walk-through completed July 14. Repaint started July 15. Carpet install scheduled July 18. On track for July 21 target." The two rows describe the same unit at different stages and the later one governs, so rec98bdfeec73545e is the record that does not line up with the ground. Note that the July dates in both notes sit after universe today 2026-07-01, so the correction rests on one row superseding the other rather than on the work being finished by now.

OE 17: Compare the four Sunset Ridge 309C rows against each other. rec987aae7d522057 is recorded selSched and its fldNotes2 poses an open question, "Alicia to confirm whether deep-clean crew is available July 21 or if we need to push to July 23", which recf50eb955a10651 answers ("Alicia confirmed deep-clean crew available July 21. Coordinating vendor access for that morning. Flooring inspection also booked for July 22") and rec2471fac3f9ae51 confirms ("July 21 and July 22 vendor schedule locked in"). The question rec987aae7d522057 records as open is answered on two later rows for the same unit, so it is superseded and out of step with the ground. As with 104B the July dates sit after universe today, so the correction rests on supersession within the table rather than on elapsed time.

OE 18: Search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMaintenanceTickets" and query "Finley" or "roof" (or similar) to find outstanding maintenance. The agent finds recb4aeaed326f156 (MT-2026-047, fldPriority selHigh, fldCompletionDate empty, so still open): "Top-floor unit at Finley portfolio property showing missing shingles and interior ceiling water staining - roof damage appears to exceed routine patching and requires professional evaluation. Flagged by Lisa Smith for priority assessment and licensed roofing contractor inspection." This is the only open repair ticket in either owner's scope, but it is not the only open ticket: rec46234590708b5c (MT-2026-0184) and recc0ecc885e9645e (DLQ-2026-0601) are both selHigh with an empty fldCompletionDate and both are Tanya Mitchell delinquency records, and neither names a unit, rec46234590708b5c saying only "unit on file" and recc0ecc885e9645e naming none, so neither can be attached to a property from the ticket alone. Reach the correspondence with search_threads using query "eviction" or "authorization" and open the result with get_thread, decoding payload.body.data. Ownership of that unit is contested in the universe and must not be asserted: Linear OPS-32 calls it "one of Harry Harris's units", while Gmail 2ae48555b3009a95 has Brooke Phillips requesting written eviction authorization for it from linda.castillo@gmail.com, and Patricia Nguyen runs the delinquency correspondence. Because Castillo is Patricia's owner and outside this task's scope, neither delinquency record may be graded as part of Harris's position. Seven rows in the table are open in total. Open status must be read from an empty fldCompletionDate because the table carries no status field, and that empty value is stored two ways, as an empty string and as null.

OE 19: Search Airtable using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMakeReady" and query "Ridgeview" to reach the second Finley property. The agent finds rec8b679d92f30753, fldUnit "Ridgeview - Roof Section (Common/Structural)", selSched, whose fldNotes2 records "Owner authorization received from Robert Finley for structural roof repair. Pete Donovan assigned as approved vendor following owner sign-off on the $8,400 estimate." Finley therefore carries two properties, Mesa Vista and Ridgeview, and the Ridgeview roof is the counterpart to the open ticket in OE 18. Reach the calendar side of this with list_events using fullText "Ridgeview" across brooke.phillips@starpm.com, teresa.wood@starpm.com and john.smith@starpm.com, and confirm each hit with get_event. Neither Ridgeview event sits on Lisa's own calendar and neither carries the words "Portfolio Review", so the persona-scoped read at OE 27 and the titled search at OE 28 both miss them. The row still reads selSched and its notes still say the work is "to be scheduled", but three independent records show it has moved well past that: the calendar carries a confirmed "Ridgeview Roof Section Repair" on 2026-06-08 which is already in the past, a confirmed "Vendor Walk-Through - Ridgeview Roof Repair Follow-Up" is booked for 2026-07-13, and invoice 2026-494 for the same $8,400.00 was issued on 2026-05-01. This row does not line up with the ground either.

OE 20: Search Airtable and Slack for water heater history using search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMaintenanceTickets" and query "water heater", and slack_search_public with query "water heater" to test the closed-work claim Lisa made in OE 8. The agent finds that no water heater record naming a property resolves to Mesa Vista or to either of Lisa's owners. A few water heater mentions name a manager's cluster rather than a property and cannot be placed either way, so the finding is that nothing supports Lisa's attribution rather than that the universe proves a negative. Two OPS-100 comments do repeat the attribution in prose, comment_5a6d779a715f587392dd00b9c8dbbd4a calling out "the water heater subfloor repair" as a pass-through in Robert's May report and comment_b575411ba2be5ceaa0ab28094905f844 answering his question on "the water heater flooring repair". Neither is independent corroboration. comment_5a6d779a715f587392dd00b9c8dbbd4a is dated 2026-05-26 and predates Lisa's own 2026-05-28 message, so the attribution enters through the report narrative rather than out of her Slack claim, and the work underneath it is the Tommy Reyes water heater that Carlos Mendez raised on 2026-05-15 and John Smith reported replacing on 2026-05-16. No maintenance ticket, invoice or make-ready row anywhere places water heater work on a Finley property. The property-named water heater records resolve to 412 Mesquite (Tommy Reyes, a Linda Castillo property), Pinecrest 12 (recb5119334a90255 and recf040e18d826352) and two still-open maintenance records rec18899b6ec2a65f and rec8c69237d76b259 on Tommy Reyes's unit. Lisa's "water heater leak" was attributed to the wrong portfolio.

OE 21: Search Gmail using search_threads with query "collections" or "past due" (or similar) and open the result with get_thread to test the collections claim. The agent finds the only past-due tenant correspondence anywhere near either owner belongs to Tanya Mitchell. Seven rows in tblMakeReady name her: "Sunset Ridge Unit 14" on reca8230a8fd9ff51, bare "Unit 14" on rec91517a5acab558 and recc83c05d889b354, "Unit 14 - Tanya Mitchell Eviction" on receee45491536859, "Tanya Mitchell - Eviction Track" on rec3782834f35df50, "Tanya Mitchell - Delinquency Escalation" on rec8005502043b755, and "Las Palmas 4B" on rec769c9f03f0b85f. An eighth row, "Rio Bend - Unit 14" on rec94e86a3007dd5e, carries the same bare unit number and does not name her. Two of her rows qualify the unit by property and they disagree, reca8230a8fd9ff51 placing her on Sunset Ridge and rec769c9f03f0b85f on Las Palmas, so Airtable alone cannot pin her to one property. What the sweep does settle is that no row places her on Mesa Vista, which is all this step needs. The same sweep establishes that no record anywhere carries a collections percentage for either portfolio, so the 97 percent figure in Lisa's claim has no source either. Gmail bodies are base64url encoded in payload.body.data, so the thread must be opened and decoded rather than read from the search snippet. Lisa's "one late payment cleared after first notice" therefore points at a different property from the one she reported it under.

OE 22: Search QuickBooks using search_customers with query "Finley" and query "Harris" to resolve the two owner records. The agent finds exactly one customer row each: Robert Finley proj-e59d4a436ed7 (robert.finley@gmail.com) and Harry Harris proj-e6adffd68bf9 (harry.harris@gmail.com). Customer rows in this universe carry only Active, CompanyName, DisplayName and PrimaryEmailAddr, so an owner balance has to be built from the transaction records rather than read off the customer.

OE 23: Search QuickBooks using search_invoices with query "Finley" (or call get_aged_receivables with customer "Robert Finley") and open each result with read_invoice. The agent finds three open invoices, all past due against universe today 2026-07-01: 109367557444 (DocNumber 2026-494, $8,400.00, TxnDate 2026-05-01, DueDate 2026-05-31, for roof section repair at the Ridgeview property), 129552155569 (DocNumber 2026-303, $2,190.00, DueDate 2026-06-05) and 793996025934 (DocNumber 4421, $390.00, DueDate 2026-06-12). A fourth invoice 110099741914 (DocNumber 5848, $640.00) is settled. Robert Finley's open receivable is $10,980.00.

OE 24: Search QuickBooks using search_invoices with query "Harris" and open each result with read_invoice. The agent finds three invoices, 317923399822 (DocNumber B2026-086, $510.00), 113714702211 (DocNumber 4422, $60.00) and 879979204592 (DocNumber 2026-057, $1,345.00), each carrying a Balance of $0.00 and each matched by a payment of the same amount. Harry Harris has no open receivable at all. Because this is a negative, the agent should confirm it with get_aged_receivables using customer "Harry Harris" rather than resting it on the absence of hits in a name search. The two owners are behind for different reasons, and reporting them as a single "both owners are behind" position loses that distinction.

OE 25: Search QuickBooks using search_credit_memos with query "Finley" and query "Harris", then confirm individual records with get_credit_memo. The agent finds three credit memos for Finley totalling $3,655.00 (920762830750 DocNumber 2026-B-317 at $2,755.00, 203129812397 DocNumber INV-2026-0718 at $490.00, 152560067925 DocNumber BILL-2026-0335 at $410.00) and three for Harris totalling $1,975.00 (390637322875 DocNumber 2026-CM-089 at $195.00, 120329707702 DocNumber INV-2026-0841-572 at $1,250.00, 262820673328 DocNumber BILL-2026-0336 at $530.00). Every one of these carries Balance equal to TotalAmt and no LinkedTxn, which is what establishes that none of them is applied against anything and none of them reduces the receivable. Each also carries a RemainingCredit of 0, which reads the other way and is the trap: taken alone it suggests the credit has been consumed. Balance and LinkedTxn govern, and all 117 credit memos in this universe share the same shape. Four of the six wear BILL- or INV- prefixes despite being credit memos, so document type must be read from the record rather than inferred from the DocNumber.

OE 26: Determine Robert Finley's actual position from OE 23 and OE 25. The receivable is $10,980.00 gross, and the $3,655.00 of credit memos sitting on his account is unapplied rather than offsetting, so netting the two to report $7,325.00 misstates what he owes. One further record has to be dispositioned before this total is stated, because it is what a property-scoped money query returns. search_invoices with query "Mesa Vista" returns exactly one record in the universe, invoice 445653930748 (DocNumber 2026-534, 1,622.00, TxnDate 2026-05-01, DueDate 2026-05-31, so 31 days past due), which bills the Mesa Vista Unit 4C post-move-out deep clean, interior repaint and closet trim touch-up. Its CustomerRef is Linda Castillo, not Robert Finley, and Gmail 66132537181ecbe1 has Carlos Mendez sending the same cost summary to her as the owner of that unit. It therefore does not belong in Finley's receivable and 10,980.00 stands, but an agent that surfaces it as a billing question on the Mesa Vista portfolio has found something real and must not be marked down for naming it, provided it is not added to Finley's balance. The money question the prompt anticipates is the $8,400.00 Ridgeview roof invoice 2026-494, which is 31 days past due and matches the same roof work Finley authorised in rec8b679d92f30753 and the open ticket MT-2026-047 from OE 18.

OE 27: Call list_calendars to enumerate the 20 calendars in the universe, then call list_events with calendarId "lisa.smith@starpm.com" to see what the persona holds. Lisa carries 16 event rows, the latest dated 2026-06-02, and none on or after universe today. Scoping the calendar to the persona alone shows one mid-year review per owner, plus a Castillo review that belongs to Patricia and a separate May owner report review for Finley. It hides the Harris duplicate entirely, because Lisa holds no row on it, so the agent must widen the search to the other attendees' calendars.

OE 28: Call list_events with fullText "Portfolio Review" across the other participants' calendars (aurora.winona@starpm.com, brooke.phillips@starpm.com, patricia.nguyen@starpm.com, teresa.wood@starpm.com) and confirm each hit with get_event. That query returns every mid-year portfolio review in the universe, including "Linda Castillo Mid-Year Portfolio Review" (base id epax0kiwoq0ygmqxezm2pax18l, 2026-05-26, Lisa holds a row and accepted). Castillo is Patricia's owner under the OE 3 split, so she is out of scope here and no conclusion in this task may rest on her. David Shea returns nothing at all, because he has no calendar presence anywhere. Within Lisa's two owners the agent finds two live events for the same owner one day apart, both status confirmed and neither cancelled: "Harry Harris Mid-Year Portfolio Review" (base id 1pon50ds1aevem63td6f7emdn3, 2026-06-02 12:15 to 12:45, five calendar rows including Lisa's row 1pon50ds1aevem63td6f7emdn3-b0504ab4, all four attendees accepted) and "Harry Harris Mid-Year Portfolio Review (Rescheduled)" (base id qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk, 2026-06-03 15:00 to 16:30, four calendar rows, Aurora Winona and Patricia Nguyen both declined, Teresa Wood accepted). Lisa holds no row on the rescheduled instance and is not an attendee of it, so it is reachable only by listing calendars she is not on.

OE 29: Call get_event with eventId "8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2-b0504ab4", which is Lisa's row on base event 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2, to check the second owner's meeting. A bare base id is not a valid eventId, because none of the 565 stored calendar rows carries one. The agent finds "Robert Finley Mid-Year Portfolio Review", base id 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2, on 2026-05-19 from 11:45 to 13:15, four rows including Lisa's row 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2-b0504ab4, with Lisa Smith and Aurora Winona both declined and Robert Finley not an attendee at all. This contradicts comment_79dc83838bd65d678c48b5911f942412 from OE 4 on three counts: the meeting is in May rather than the first week of June, it runs 90 minutes rather than 60, and it sits in late morning rather than the afternoon. The Harris original carries the same defect against its own announcement, because Slack described it as a casual 45-minute morning call in late June while the event is 30 minutes at midday on 2026-06-02. A sweep of 2026-06-01 through 2026-06-09 returns no Finley event, so the meeting that comment describes does not exist. A separate event, "May Owner Report Review - Finley Properties" (base id ti5zt1xubdggbehtp79um9mim6, 2026-05-28 11:45 to 12:15, three rows, Lisa declined, Robert Finley accepted), is also a Finley review meeting that also looks unsettled, and it must not be mistaken for the mid-year review: it is the monthly May report review, a different deliverable. Neither owner's mid-year review ended up properly settled.

OE 30: Update the make-ready records that do not match the ground, using update_records_for_table with baseId "appPropertyOps", tableId "tblMakeReady" and a records array. Three rows across the two owners qualify, and they are exactly the selSched rows that the surrounding record has moved past, whether because the work has started or because the question that row was waiting on has been answered and the vendor schedule locked in. The agent moves rec98bdfeec73545e (Sunset Ridge 104B) to selProg because a later row on the same unit records the walk-through done and the repaint and carpet scoped, rec987aae7d522057 (Sunset Ridge 309C) to selProg because the question it records as open is answered on two later rows for the same unit, and rec8b679d92f30753 (Ridgeview) to selProg because the repair event has already happened and the work is invoiced. Mesa Vista 207A and Mesa Vista 4C each carry selProg rows alongside a selReady row stating the unit is finished and cleared for leasing. Neither pair is graded, and the two have separate reasons. On 4C the weight of the record supports the selReady row rather than contradicting it, and most of that record sits outside tblMakeReady, so it has to be retrieved deliberately: search_records with baseId "appPropertyOps", table "tblMaintenanceTickets" and query "Mesa Vista" returns the turn tickets, search_threads with query "make-ready" returns the owner thread and get_thread opens it, search_bills with query "Mesa Vista" or "4C" returns the vendor bills, and list_events across carlos.mendez@starpm.com and wesley.tran@starpm.com returns the QC inspection, which carries none of the query terms used at OE 19, OE 27 or OE 28 and sits on no calendar Lisa holds. What those calls return: closed ticket reca424761ae15355 in tblMaintenanceTickets reports "All make-ready work at Mesa Vista 4C is complete" and that unit status was updated to market-ready, Gmail 66132537181ecbe1 has Carlos Mendez telling the owner the unit is market-ready and handed to leasing, and four QuickBooks vendor bills 195089456477, 696089964235, 546359391323 and 991582431419 are entered for the deep clean, the repaint, the closet trim touch-up and the punch list. One record points the other way, confirmed calendar event 0hjw400xgjb3j7ay7ynuaqbnpi on 2026-07-15, carried on brooke.phillips@starpm.com, carlos.mendez@starpm.com and wesley.tran@starpm.com at location "Mesa Vista, Unit 4C", a "Final make-ready QC inspection" whose description is to "confirm the unit is rent-ready and release it to leasing", which stands as booked work after the date the record says the unit was released. That single event is not enough to overturn the other six, and the unit's ownership is itself contested, because rec12969a3fdb0852 flags Linda Castillo on the turn and QuickBooks invoice 445653930748 bills the 4C pass-throughs to her rather than to Robert Finley while Gmail 66132537181ecbe1 is addressed to her as the owner. Castillo is Patricia's owner under the OE 3 split, so 4C stays out of the graded set on the same ground as the Unit 14 delinquency records at OE 18, and an agent that leaves the pair alone, an agent that moves recbd087a4abd605b forward, and an agent that moves recc8534b3fd13954 back are all acceptable. On 207A no record on any other service names the unit at all, so the only reading available is that the later row supersedes the earlier ones, and with no cross-service check on it neither direction of correction can be graded. As with 4C, an agent that leaves the 207A rows alone and an agent that corrects them are both acceptable. None of this disturbs the three graded rows. Two of them are corrected on the strength of a later row on the same unit in the same table, rec98bdfeec73545e against rec7d202aed68c95c and rec987aae7d522057 against recf50eb955a10651 and rec2471fac3f9ae51. The third, rec8b679d92f30753, is the only Ridgeview row in the table and has no sibling to read against, so it is corrected on records outside Airtable instead, confirmed calendar event whd6zys0hw7zbsh11m9vqv4m4i on 2026-06-08 for the repair itself and invoice 109367557444 for the completed work. What the three share, and what separates them from these two pairs, is that each correction runs forward out of selSched into selProg and no record anywhere stands against that direction. No record outside Airtable carries 309C at all, the only rows outside it carrying 104B sit on Fernwood Gardens rather than Sunset Ridge, and the one later Ridgeview event, the 2026-07-13 close-out walk-through on 42b119cbt7xd0vnhw6dwvdqizo, verifies work already done and sits consistently with a turn that is in progress rather than finished. The other three selSched rows in these clusters must also be left alone: rec88734a4fdfde57 (Mesa Vista 310C) is waiting on a July 16 inspection that has not happened, reca06d89f1a4ac5b (Sunset Ridge 309C) is waiting on a utility transfer that no record resolves, and reca8230a8fd9ff51 (Sunset Ridge Unit 14) is conditional on two things together, the balance remaining unresolved and the unit becoming vacant. The balance limb has been met, because rec8005502043b755 records the plan updated to Payment Plan Breached after the June 23 installment went unmet, rec3782834f35df50 records that the tenant did not cure before the June 29 deadline, and Gmail 2ae48555b3009a95 confirms the cure deadline expired with no payment. The vacancy limb has not, and possession has not been returned, so the turn is still contingent. It stays out of the graded set on that ground and on the contested ownership described at OE 18, and an agent that leaves it alone and an agent that advances it are both acceptable. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (rec98bdfeec73545e set to selProg, rec987aae7d522057 set to selProg, rec8b679d92f30753 set to selProg) rather than one criterion covering the set.

OE 31: Resolve both owners' review meetings on the calendar. Both are required, because the prompt's condition fires on each of them independently. For Harris, the rescheduled instance is the one to act on, and because Lisa holds no row on it she cannot respond to it. She addresses it with delete_event or update_event using the eventId that list_events returned for it, which is a per-calendar row such as qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk-b6a1e41c on teresa.wood@starpm.com or qqbwq3s2h7wh5udoek2940mffk-0f82233a on brooke.phillips@starpm.com, not the bare base id. Either verb is correct provided the rescheduled instance is cancelled or retitled on the calendar the agent addresses, so that it no longer stands as a second live Harris review alongside the 2026-06-02 original. For Finley, the review sat on 2026-05-19 and is six weeks in the past, so responding to it settles nothing. The agent addresses row 8mwlxrq5w5oodwdpmvo83e00f2-b0504ab4 with update_event to move it to a workable date, or uses create_event to put a replacement review on the calendar. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (the duplicated Harris review no longer stands as two live meetings, the Finley review is rescheduled or replaced).

OE 32: Look up the hand-off recipient using contacts_search_contacts with query "Brooke Phillips". The agent resolves contact_id c46d47256fd95ca6aca770c8dddda5eb, email brooke.phillips@starpm.com, job "Apartment Property Supervisor". There is exactly one Brooke Phillips in contacts, in Slack and in HubSpot, so the recipient is unambiguous.

OE 33: Create the hand-off email using create_draft with to ["brooke.phillips@starpm.com"], a subject naming the mid-year review hand-off for Harry Harris and Robert Finley, and a body carrying the specifics for both owners. Gmail in this universe is draft-only and there is no send tool, so the deliverable is the draft itself. The body must separate the two owners: Harris is operationally blocked, with every make-ready row on his Sunset Ridge units sitting in Scheduled or In Progress and none in a Ready state, against $0.00 of open receivable; Finley is cash-blocked, with $10,980.00 past due across invoices 2026-494, 2026-303 and 4421 plus $3,655.00 of credit memos that are unapplied and therefore do not reduce the balance, and an open roof ticket MT-2026-047. The body must also correct the spring read: the 94 percent occupancy and 97 percent collections figures have no supporting record, Mesa Vista carries make-ready rows on four units rather than one, of which at least two are still open on their latest row, so any count from two to four is correct provided the agent states that more than one unit is involved, the closed water heater ticket belongs to a different portfolio, and nothing in the records supports a cleared late payment for either portfolio: the only past-due tenant anywhere near these owners is Tanya Mitchell, whose balance was never cleared but ran from first notice on June 6 through a breached payment plan on June 25 to a cure deadline that expired on June 29 with no payment. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (recipient, Harris make-ready position, Harris receivable position, Finley receivable total, Finley unapplied credits, Finley open maintenance, the occupancy and collections correction, the make-ready count correction, the water heater correction, the late-payment correction).

OE 34: Post the progress update on the parent review item using save_comment with issueId "OPS-10" and a body describing where Lisa's half has landed. The comment covers both owners, the corrections made to the make-ready records, the calendar resolution, and the fact that the hand-off has gone to Brooke. The agent may also move OPS-10 out of state_OPS_0 using save_issue, since the issue's own comment thread has claimed two transitions that never took effect. That state change is optional and must not be graded: OPS-10 is the four-owner parent and Patricia's half is not in scope here, so an agent that leaves the state alone is equally correct.

OE 35: Open one new issue for the work that survives the hand-off using save_issue with title naming the unresolved item, team "team_001", project "proj_002" and a description. The item is the Mesa Vista 310C subfloor assessment from OE 15. The prompt asks for one item so the work does not quietly disappear at hand-off. MT-2026-047 already exists as a ticket and the Ridgeview follow-up already has a booked walk-through on 2026-07-13, so neither of those needs a new item to survive. Two candidates are carried nowhere, the Mesa Vista 310C subfloor assessment and the Sunset Ridge 309C utility transfer confirmation on reca06d89f1a4ac5b. The subfloor assessment is the expected target because it blocks a turn scope outright, and an agent that raises the 309C blocker instead has picked a defensible target and must not be marked down. Exactly one issue is expected, matching the prompt's "a separate item". The created issue's own identifier cannot be predicted because team_001 has next_issue_number 1000, so it must be graded on title and description rather than on a number. S3 must write this as a single criterion whose accept-set covers either the Mesa Vista 310C subfloor assessment or the Sunset Ridge 309C utility transfer confirmation, graded on title and description.

OE 36: Post the short version to the team using slack_send_message with channel_id "C006" and message summarising where Lisa's two owners sit. Brooke's own 2026-05-28 posts in that channel establish the precedent for owner review status landing there as a top-level message. The post is a condensed version of the email: the two owners named, the Harris turn position, the Finley past-due position, the one item still open, and the fact that the hand-off has gone to Brooke. S3 must decompose this into one criterion per content element (post lands in C006, both owners named, Finley past-due position stated, the open item named).
